<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="Every reason a figure on this site does not say what it looks like it says, in full, with the document each one belongs to.">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<script>
  // Stamp the saved theme before first paint, the same way index.html does, so
  // a reader who chose dark there does not get a white flash here. This page
  // ships no app.js, so this is the whole of its scripting.
  try {
    var t = localStorage.getItem("fisc-theme");
    if (t === "light" || t === "dark") document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t;
  } catch (e) { /* private mode: fall back to the OS setting */ }
</script>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wip-banner" role="status">⚠️ Work in progress — early preview, coverage is incomplete.</div>

{{- if gt (len .Nav) 1}}
<nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Views">
  <ul>
    {{- range .Nav}}
    <li>{{if .Current}}<span aria-current="page">{{.Label}}</span>{{else}}<a href="{{.Path}}">{{.Label}}</a>{{end}}</li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
</nav>
{{- end}}

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <h1>{{.Title}}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{{.Lede}}</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

<!-- "THE DOCUMENTS THAT HAVE A PAGE" IS NOT ALL OF THEM, and the lede used to
     imply it was: it said every other page links here for the rest, while three
     of the seven documents listed below ship as data no page draws and so have
     no inbound link at all. Their caveats are still owed to anyone who fetches
     those files, which is why they are here; saying so is cheaper than a
     reader noticing the asymmetry and wondering what else is unstated. -->
<p class="lede">
  {{.Count}} caveat{{if ne .Count 1}}s{{end}} across {{len .Documents}} published document{{if ne (len .Documents) 1}}s{{end}}.
  The pages that draw a document show its caveats one line at a time and link here for the
  rest; this is the rest. Documents the site publishes as data but does not yet draw are
  listed too, with no page pointing at them — their caveats are owed to whoever fetches the
  file, and repeat what a drawn year of the same schedule says.
</p>

<!-- GROUPED BY DOCUMENT, and the reason is not tidiness. One caveat id can
     carry different text in different documents: the transfer-legs caveat has
     three sentences, chosen by whether that column's legs balance and whether
     its residual meets the figure the city prints, and the FY2026-27 spine
     carries a contested-total caveat FY2025-26 does not. A page keyed on the id
     alone would have to pick one of those texts and would be wrong about the
     others, so the anchor is per (document, caveat) and so is this heading. -->
{{- range $doc := .Documents}}
<section class="caveat-doc">
  <h2>{{.Stem}}{{if .Label}} <span class="subtle">— {{.Label}}</span>{{end}}</h2>
  <p class="subtle">
    {{len .Entries}} caveat{{if ne (len .Entries) 1}}s{{end}}, shipped inside
    <a href="{{.DataPath}}">{{.DataPath}}</a> rather than only stated here.
  </p>
  {{- range .Entries}}
  <article class="caveat" id="{{.Anchor}}">
    <h3><a href="#{{.Anchor}}" class="anchor-link">{{.Summary}}</a></h3>
    <p>{{.Text}}</p>
    {{- if .AppliesTo}}
    {{- /* THE CHARTS DO MARK THESE NOW (fisc-yj4w.8), which an earlier version
           of this comment said they did not: app.js's caveatsFor resolves each
           id through the hierarchy, so a caveat about fund/100 chips that node
           where it is drawn and fund-group/general where it folds.

           CONDITIONAL ON THE DOCUMENT HAVING A PAGE, because three of the four
           fund-flows columns do not -- unviewedDocuments declares them as data
           no page draws, and this page's own lede says so twelve lines above.
           Promising a flag on a chart that does not exist is the same class as
           the sentence this hunk replaced.

           The wording stays "is about" rather than "marks" either way, because
           it is true of a reader who never opens a chart -- and because the ids
           are checked against the document by project.ValidateCaveats, which is
           what makes the sentence trustworthy whether or not any page draws
           them. */}}
    <p class="subtle">
      About
      {{- range $i, $n := .AppliesTo}}{{if $i}},{{end}} <code>{{$n}}</code>{{end}}
      in this document{{if $doc.Drawn}} — the charts flag these marks where they draw them{{end}}.
    </p>
    {{- end}}
  </article>
  {{- end}}
</section>
{{- end}}

</main>

<footer>
  <!-- NO SOURCES LIST, and that is deliberate rather than missing. Every other
       page's footer cites the pages its figures were read from; this page shows
       no figures, so the union of every document's pages would be the broadest
       false provenance claim on the site. Each document links to itself above. -->
  {{- /* SO THIS DISCLOSURE HOLDS ONLY THE FILE LIST, and its id says so. Every
         other footer's holds a source list beside it; there is none to put
         here, for the reason stated directly above. */}}
  <details class="apparatus" id="data-view">
    <summary>
      <h3>The data</h3>
      <span class="apparatus-hint">{{len .Projections}} file{{if ne (len .Projections) 1}}s{{end}},
        each fetchable on its own</span>
    </summary>
    <p class="subtle">
      Every data file the site publishes:
      {{- range $i, $p := .Projections}}{{if $i}},{{end}} <a href="{{$p.Path}}">{{$p.Path}}</a>{{end}}.
    </p>
  </details>
  <p class="subtle">
    Packaged by {{.ExportedBy}}. This page draws no projection and publishes no figure: it
    is what the other pages' figures do not say, collected so a reader can hold the whole set
    at once. Each caveat ships inside the document it belongs to, so it travels with the data
    rather than only with the page.
  </p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
